{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgjZzUV4939MRWbUVUURV","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01D2K8dbGo8UnwxTaFvjk9bf","name":"StructuredOutput","input":{"working":"Start: G clef, 3/4. Three 8ths marked \"piani\" form the upbeat C5 B4 C5. That is bar 0. Bar 1: a 16th group followed by 8ths, matching D E D C D then D C D. Bar 2: grace note, quarter, then 8ths, matching grace D5, E5 quarter, C5, then E D E. Bar 3: 16ths then 8ths, matching F G F E F then F E F. Bar 4: one 8th, two 8th rests, then three 8ths, matching G5, rests, then D E F. Bar 5: six 8ths in two groups, matching D E C D B C. Bar 6: grace, quarter, 8th, then a run of six 16ths under a slur marked \"poco for.\", matching grace B4, A4, G4, then G A B C D E with \"poco f\". Bar 7: three 8ths, then a 16th group (D C B A), matching D D D D, then D C B A. After the next barline comes a short stretch: two low notes, a quarter-length and an 8th-length value on G4. That is bar 8's G4 quarter tied to a G4 8th. It ends at the double bar with repeat dots, which is the repeat sign inside bar 8. Bar 8's second half (p, G G G) must start the next line. So the line holds bars 0 to 8, ending partway through bar 8.","right":false,"insights":"Most bars were easy to match. The upbeat, the grace-plus-quarter bars and \"poco for.\" pin down the alignment. The hard part was the short half-bar before the repeat sign at the line's end. It is easy to read as a final bar 7 cadence instead of the start of bar 8. If the edition's text said where the repeat falls inside bar 8, after the tied G4, the split would be obvious."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":5506,"cache_read_input_tokens":523,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":5506},"output_tokens":5,"service_tier":"standard","inference_ge